00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.556
This is currently in development.
The key insight here is that each phase builds on the last.

00:05:02.036 --> 00:05:08.116
You can build a great agent without great search and you
can improve search without a great data foundation.

00:05:08.796 --> 00:05:10.716
So let's dive into each one.

00:05:13.076 --> 00:05:22.716
Let's dive deeper into legal search, which is already
in production in.com. At its core, this is a Q&A legal search system.

00:05:23.196 --> 00:05:25.756
A lawyer can type any natural language question.

00:05:25.756 --> 00:05:30.196
For example, what are the elements of Singapore
of negligence in Singapore law?

00:05:30.636 --> 00:05:38.116
And the system returns an AI generated answer with legal citations,
plus a ranked list of retrieved source documents.

00:05:38.796 --> 00:05:41.636
The key capabilities are natural language queries.

00:05:41.876 --> 00:05:45.156
So no more Boolean search strings. Cited answers.

00:05:45.316 --> 00:05:50.156
So every claim is traceable back to a source
and full source document retrieval.

00:05:50.596 --> 00:05:52.676
So users can verify and go deeper.

00:05:53.796 --> 00:05:58.436
One thing I want you to highlight or want
to highlight is our users controls.

00:05:59.396 --> 00:06:03.476
We implement rate limiting five queries per five minutes per user.

00:06:04.036 --> 00:06:07.476
This isn't just about cost management in the legal domain.

00:06:07.476 --> 00:06:15.996
Responsible usage matters. So we want lawyers to use
this as a research aid and not a replacement for legal judgment.

00:06:16.436 --> 00:06:18.916
It's about building trust with the profession.

00:06:20.596 --> 00:06:23.916
Here is what the experience actually looks like for our users.

00:06:24.236 --> 00:06:27.356
You can see the familiar search bar at the top.

00:06:27.796 --> 00:06:35.876
Users can toggle between classic search, the traditional keyword
based search, and lon et ai, which is our new AI powered search.

00:06:36.596 --> 00:06:40.636
When a user submits a query, they get an AI generated answer,

00:06:40.636 --> 00:06:48.516
citing the paragraph across cases and legislation to help lawyers
quickly assess whether a judgment is relevant to their research.

00:06:49.556 --> 00:06:55.916
This combination of AI generated answers
with citations to sources has been critical for adoption.

00:06:56.836 --> 00:07:01.596
Lawyers don't just want answers,
they want to know why they should trust those answers.

00:07:04.476 --> 00:07:07.596
Here you see the architecture that powers all of this.

00:07:07.596 --> 00:07:14.476
There are many services used for storage,
catching, and API, but I would like to highlight three of them.

00:07:16.196 --> 00:07:25.956
First, in the middle layer, we have X hosted models for embedding
and inference and natural language processing service,

00:07:25.956 --> 00:07:30.956
and the Q&A backend that orchestrates
the search and answer generation pipelines.

00:07:30.956 --> 00:07:34.596
We host the GPT models that we co-developed with Lambda

00:07:35.076 --> 00:07:40.636
and the natural language processing models
on accelerated compute instances in X containers.

00:07:42.516 --> 00:07:47.436
Next, we use Amazon Bedrock Guardrails for responsible AI checks

00:07:49.316 --> 00:07:56.196
and at the heart of the search Amazon OpenSearch
service for both full text and vector search.

00:07:58.396 --> 00:08:01.516
The key design principle here is separation of concerns.

00:08:02.076 --> 00:08:07.436
Each component does one thing well,
and they communicate through well-defined interfaces.

00:08:08.076 --> 00:08:12.116
This makes the system easy to maintain, scale, and evolve.

00:08:12.596 --> 00:08:17.476
This, which becomes critical when
we get to the Agentic AI phase later.

00:08:19.916 --> 00:08:22.236
Now for the search and ingestion workflow.

00:08:22.236 --> 00:08:24.796
Starting at the bottom with the document ingestion.

00:08:24.796 --> 00:08:31.596
When a new legal document judgment or legislation arrives,
it goes through text and metadata extraction.

00:08:32.036 --> 00:08:38.036
Then judgment the duplication because the same case
can have different citation across their life cycle,

00:08:38.396 --> 00:08:41.756
from a reported judgment to Singapore Law report, or SLR.

00:08:43.236 --> 00:08:49.276
Then the extracted data goes through an enrichment
pipeline where we normalize legislation and case citations,

00:08:49.756 --> 00:08:54.956
then generate embeddings using our fine
tuned model and extract reference trace metadata.

00:08:55.716 --> 00:09:00.276
The output is stored in both a vector
database and a reference database.

00:09:01.876 --> 00:09:03.876
Now for the query workflow at the top.

00:09:03.876 --> 00:09:08.716
When a user submits a question,
it first goes through a query preprocessing.

00:09:08.996 --> 00:09:13.916
This means from validation entity extraction and entity normalization.

00:09:14.476 --> 00:09:18.356
This produces a query that captures the legal intent.

00:09:19.436 --> 00:09:23.076
That query fits into our enhanced search phase.

00:09:23.756 --> 00:09:26.436
Hybrid search execution followed by Reranking.

00:09:27.156 --> 00:09:32.076
The top results. Then go to the inference
phase where we generate the answer with context,

00:09:32.436 --> 00:09:37.956
then apply Guardrails and return the answer with citations,
plus a list of retrieved cases.

00:09:39.276 --> 00:09:41.436
What I want you to notice is a symmetry.

00:09:42.036 --> 00:09:48.676
The same entities we extract and normalize
during ingestion are the ones that we extract from queries.

00:09:49.396 --> 00:09:52.516
This alignment is what makes the search accurate.

00:09:55.916 --> 00:10:00.000
Let me go deeper on the surface layer,
because this is where a lot of the magic happens.

